Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and why they matter  
• Match.id – not needed for the logic itself, but handy when checking individual rows.  
• Match.league_id – tells us which league every match belongs to.  
• Match.home_team_api_id / Match.away_team_api_id – identify the two teams that played.  
• Match.home_team_goal / Match.away_team_goal – let us decide who won the match.  
• Team.team_api_id – joins a team appearing in Match to the Team table.  
• Team.team_long_name – readable team name for the final output.  
• League.id – joins to Match.league_id.  
• League.name – readable league name for the final output.  

General conditions / ideas we will use  
• A team wins a match if it scores strictly more goals than the opponent (home > away or away > home).  
• We need to count wins for every (league, team) pair over ALL seasons.  
• Teams that never win must still appear with 0 wins, so we accumulate one row per team per match with a win‑flag 0/1, then SUM it.  
• For ties on the fewest wins inside a league we keep only one team (the smallest team_api_id is an easy deterministic tiebreaker).  

Below are 10 progressively richer inspection / build‑up SQL statements that finally lead to the answer.  
Every query is capped with LIMIT 20 to stay light on the DB, and each is preceded by a short comment.

```sql
-- 1) Peek at a handful of matches to see the raw goal columns
SELECT id, league_id, home_team_api_id, away_team_api_id,
       home_team_goal, away_team_goal
FROM Match
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Which leagues exist?  (ID -> name)
SELECT id, name
FROM League
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) A sampling of team IDs and readable names
SELECT team_api_id, team_long_name
FROM Team
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) List the matches where the home side actually won
SELECT id,
       home_team_api_id AS winner_team_id,
       league_id
FROM Match
WHERE home_team_goal > away_team_goal
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Same idea but for away‑side wins
SELECT id,
       away_team_api_id AS winner_team_id,
       league_id
FROM Match
WHERE away_team_goal > home_team_goal
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Build one row per team per match with a 0/1 win flag (home side only)
SELECT league_id,
       home_team_api_id AS team_id,
       CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
FROM Match
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Union the home view and the away view to cover both sides
SELECT *
FROM (
      SELECT league_id,
             home_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
      UNION ALL
      SELECT league_id,
             away_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
     )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Aggregate the previous derived set to get total wins per (league, team)
WITH all_matches AS (
      SELECT league_id,
             home_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
      UNION ALL
      SELECT league_id,
             away_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
)
SELECT league_id,
       team_id,
       SUM(win_flag) AS wins
FROM all_matches
GROUP BY league_id, team_id
ORDER BY league_id, wins
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) For every league find the minimum number of wins
WITH all_matches AS (
      SELECT league_id,
             home_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
      UNION ALL
      SELECT league_id,
             away_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
),
team_wins AS (
      SELECT league_id, team_id, SUM(win_flag) AS wins
      FROM all_matches
      GROUP BY league_id, team_id
)
SELECT league_id,
       MIN(wins) AS fewest_wins
FROM team_wins
GROUP BY league_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL ANSWER:
--     For each league return ONE team (tie‑broken by smallest team_api_id)
--     that has the fewest total wins across all seasons.
WITH all_matches AS (
      SELECT league_id,
             home_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
      UNION ALL
      SELECT league_id,
             away_team_api_id AS team_id,
             CASE WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS win_flag
      FROM Match
),
team_wins AS (
      SELECT league_id, team_id, SUM(win_flag) AS wins
      FROM all_matches
      GROUP BY league_id, team_id
),
league_min AS (
      SELECT league_id, MIN(wins) AS fewest_wins
      FROM team_wins
      GROUP BY league_id
),
candidates AS (
      SELECT tw.league_id, tw.team_id, tw.wins
      FROM team_wins  AS tw
      JOIN league_min AS lm
        ON tw.league_id = lm.league_id
       AND tw.wins     = lm.fewest_wins
),
one_per_league AS (
      -- deterministic tiebreaker: keep the numerically smallest team_id
      SELECT league_id,
             MIN(team_id) AS team_id,
             MAX(wins) AS wins      -- wins are all equal here (=fewest_wins)
      FROM candidates
      GROUP BY league_id
)
SELECT  l.name  AS league_name,
        t.team_long_name AS team_with_fewest_wins,
        o.wins AS total_wins
FROM    one_per_league AS o
JOIN    League        AS l ON l.id          = o.league_id
JOIN    Team          AS t ON t.team_api_id = o.team_id
ORDER BY league_name
LIMIT 20;
```

Running the last statement will give, for every league in the database, exactly one team that owned the lowest number of wins (even zero) across all available seasons.
